"""The About and sources page: what the map shows, how the scores are built, how to read
each layer, and where the data come from. Written as static HTML with the counts filled in
at build time."""
import html as H


def about(data):
    rows, cases, orgs, groups, routes, pats = (data['rows'], data['cases'], data['entities'], data['groups'],
                                               data['routes'], data['patterns'])
    n = len(rows)
    sev = sum(1 for r in rows.values() if r['exp'] >= 7.5)
    est = sum(1 for r in rows.values() if r['est'])
    meta = data['meta']
    bys = {}
    for c in cases:
        bys[c['status']] = bys.get(c['status'], 0) + 1
    order = ['convicted', 'inquiry', 'acknowledged', 'settled', 'designated', 'indicted', 'reported', 'annulled', 'acquitted']
    status_line = ', '.join('%d %s' % (bys[s], s) for s in order if s in bys)
    byb = ', '.join('%d %s' % (sum(1 for c in cases if c['bk'] == b[0]), b[1].lower()) for b in data['casebuckets'])
    byt = ', '.join('%s (%d)' % (t['label'].lower(), sum(1 for o in orgs if o['s'] == t['k'])) for t in data['sectors'])
    ncty = len({c['c'] for c in cases})
    ndoc = sum(1 for g in groups if not g['est'])
    p = []
    a = p.append
    a('<h2>What the map shows</h2>')
    a('<p>Nobody keeps a register of which officials belong to which society, and most of these organisations exist so that no such register can be compiled. Membership lists surface when a police raid finds one, as at Licio Gelli\'s villa in 1981; when a parliament orders one handed over, as Britain\'s did for Masonic police officers in 1999; or when a government falls and the next one opens the files, as in Turkey after 2016. So this map does not claim to count infiltration. It carries layers that can each be checked, and keeps them apart.</p>')
    a('<p>The organisations are not only secret societies. They cover every kind of international body counted in the Union of International Associations\' <a href="https://uia.org/yearbook/overview" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Yearbook of International Organizations</a> that has reached inside a government: lodges and sworn fraternities, religious orders and movements, policy clubs, covert and stay-behind networks, banks used for hidden money, networks run by one state inside another, foundations and intelligence fronts, and intergovernmental bodies such as Interpol and the WHO.</p>')
    a('<p>One case shows the standard. Roberto Calvi, chairman of Banco Ambrosiano and a member of the P2 lodge, was found hanging under Blackfriars Bridge in London in June 1982. Rome magistrates concluded in 2002 that he had been murdered; five people were tried for it and acquitted in 2007. Ambrosiano collapsed with about $1.3 billion missing, much of it lent to shell companies the Vatican Bank stood behind, and in 1984 the Vatican Bank paid $224 million to the creditors, citing "moral involvement" and admitting no liability.</p>')
    a('<h2>How to use it</h2><ul>'
      '<li><b>Patterns behind the scenes</b> (top of the key): %d arrangements that recur across the cases. Choosing one colours each country by the role it plays, draws the documented connections between places, keeps only that pattern\'s cases and organisations on the map, and opens a card with the facts, a timeline, cases per decade, what is not proven, the sources, and what it means.</li>'
      '<li><b>Countries</b>: click one for its scores, a behind-the-scenes write-up ending in what it means, the patterns it is part of, the organisations present, and every documented case.</li>'
      '<li><b>Diamonds</b> are documented cases, coloured by the office reached. <b>Circles with a pale rim</b> are organisations at their headquarters. <b>Small dots</b> are national branches; a hollow ring is a presence that is likely but not documented. <b>Dashed lines</b> are documented channels.</li>'
      '<li><b>Time</b>: the bars count cases per decade. Click one to keep a decade, or type a range.</li>'
      '<li>The <b>basemap</b> box switches between five painted and photographic earths and between the globe and a flat map. Colours on the map change with the basemap so that they stay readable on each.</li></ul>' % len(pats))
    a('<h2>Layer 1: the country score, %d countries</h2>' % n)
    a('<p><b>Documented record</b>: for each case in the country, a weight for how far it got (conviction 1.0; inquiry finding or acknowledgement 0.9; designation or settlement 0.8; indictment 0.7; press or academic report 0.45; annulled 0.3; acquitted 0.2) times a weight for the office reached (head of state 1.0; minister 0.85; security services and courts 0.8; central bank and finance 0.75; legislature and local office 0.65), times a weight for how recent it is, because this is a map of the present (2010 on 1.0; 1990–2009 0.75; 1945–89 0.5; before 1945 0.3). The sum is put on a 0–10 log scale with 10 at the 95th percentile (%s), so one country cannot flatten the rest.</p>' % meta.get('dcap'))
    a('<p><b>Organisational reach</b>: the tracked organisations with a documented national presence, each weighted by type (covert and state-run networks 1.2; lodges and orders 1.0; foundations 0.9; forums and law networks 0.8; international bodies 0.6). A likely but undocumented presence counts at half. Scaled 0–10 against the 92nd percentile.</p>')
    a('<p><b>Conditions</b>: the mean of two <a href="https://ocindex.net" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Global Organized Crime Index 2025</a> indicators, state-embedded actors and resilience (inverted). It is the one independent, expert-scored series covering all UN member states that measures officials acting for interests outside the state. It was built for organised crime, not secret societies, and is used only for what it measures: how much room any hidden network would have. Taiwan and the Holy See are not scored by the Index; Taiwan borrows South Korea\'s figures and the Holy See Italy\'s, and their cards say so.</p>')
    a('<p><b>Composite</b> = 0.5 × documented + 0.3 × reach + 0.2 × conditions. <b>%d of %d</b> countries are severe. <b>%d</b> have no documented case, so their composite is reach and conditions alone: a best-judgement estimate of where infiltration is likely. Their cards are marked Estimate, and because the documented half is zero they cannot score above 5.</p>' % (sev, n, est))
    a('<p class="warn">The score is not a count of infiltrated institutions. The documented half measures what forums have found, which tracks how free a country\'s parliament, courts and press are as much as how penetrated its government is. Italy scores high partly because its parliament published the P2 list.</p>')
    a('<h2>Layer 2: documented cases, %d in %d jurisdictions</h2>' % (len(cases), ncty))
    a('<p>Each case is a named event in which an organisation had members or agents inside a public institution, with the office reached and the forum that established it. By office: %s. By status: %s.</p>' % (byb, status_line))
    a('<p><b>Convicted, acquitted, annulled</b>: a court ruled. <b>Indicted</b>: charges filed, nothing tested. <b>Inquiry</b>: a parliamentary or official commission reported a finding. <b>Designated</b>: a government banned the organisation or expelled people for belonging to it, an executive act that some governments have used against critics. <b>Acknowledged</b>: the organisation or the government admitted it. <b>Settled</b>: money paid, liability usually denied. <b>Reported</b>: documented by major outlets or historians only, weighted at less than half a conviction. Where a claim rests on one author (Carroll Quigley, John Perkins), the case says so.</p>')
    a('<p>Where a case also appears in the Welcome to Your Galaxy conspiracy archive, its card gives the archive entry and the archive\'s trust rating.</p>')
    a('<p class="warn">Membership is not misconduct. Most people named in these cases belonged to lawful organisations. A case is here because a forum found that the organisation, through them, reached into an office.</p>')
    a('<h2>Layer 3: organisations, %d</h2><p>Each is plotted at its headquarters. Types: %s. Each card gives where its presence data come from and lists every case it has on the map.</p>' % (len(orgs), byt))
    a('<h2>Layer 4: branches and chapters, %d dots</h2><p>One dot per organisation per country of national presence: a grand lodge, a national church, a regional office, a member delegation, a school network. %d are documented; %d are estimates drawn as hollow rings, where no published list confirms a presence but the organisation\'s regional pattern makes one likely. Dots sit on a small fixed ring around the capital so that many organisations in one capital stay clickable; a dot marks a country, not an address. Presence is the channel infiltration would use, not evidence that it happened.</p>' % (len(groups), ndoc, len(groups) - ndoc))
    a('<h2>Layer 5: channels, %d</h2><p>Each joins the places named in a record — a head office and the subsidiaries it moved money through, a command centre and the services it coordinated, a church and the parliament it lobbied — along the shortest path over the globe. It shows that something moved between the places, not the route it took.</p>' % len(routes))
    a('<h2>Patterns behind the scenes</h2><p>Each pattern is written from the cases and organisations above: it names the arrangement, gives the countries and the role each plays, draws the documented connections, counts the cases by decade, lists what is not proven, and closes with what it means for someone who has not followed all of it. The country cards carry the same reading for each country.</p><ul>' +
      ''.join('<li><b>%s</b> — %d countries, %d cases, %s–%s</li>' % (H.escape(x['title']), len(x['cmap']), x['ncases'], x['span'][0], x['span'][1]) for x in pats) + '</ul>')
    a('<h2>The live wire</h2><p>A harvester in the maps repository reads Google News in eleven languages every six hours and keeps an item only if it names a tracked organisation, a public institution, and an act tying the two together. Headlines are leads, not findings; nothing in the wire enters the scores or the cases until it earns a forum and a citation.</p>')
    a('<h2>Sources</h2><p>Conditions: Global Organized Crime Index 2025, Global Initiative Against Transnational Organized Crime. Boundaries: the Culprits map\'s boundary file. Capitals: countryinfo. Basemaps: the Culprits map\'s five basemaps (Esri World Imagery, NASA Black Marble, Mapterhorn and AWS terrain tiles, OpenMapTiles, CARTO labels) and its painted Atlas plate. Case, organisation and pattern sources are on each card. Research covered court judgments, parliamentary and official inquiries (the Anselmi Commission, the Belgian and Swiss stay-behind inquiries, the US Church Committee, the Fraser subcommittee, the Kerry–Brown BCCI report, the UK Home Affairs Committee, Canada\'s Hogue inquiry), the organisations\' own published lists, Carroll Quigley\'s two books, the Council on Foreign Relations\' centennial history, and major newspapers.</p>')
    return '\n'.join(p)
